#. type: Title #
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:1 src/en/reference/privacy.md:6
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Data Retention and External Services"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:10
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"How long does Zammad store user data? How can I manage its user data "
"retention behavior? To which services connects Zammad?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:11
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Data Retention"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:14
#, markdown-text
msgid "The following data is stored locally on the production system:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:15
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tickets and Users"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:18
#, markdown-text
msgid "By default, Zammad never automatically deletes tickets or users."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:22
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To enable **automatic** deletion of tickets after a given interval,\n"
"use Zammad's scheduler. You can configure it in Zammad's admin interface "
"under\n"
"*Manage > Scheduler*.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:27
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To **manually** delete users and all their associated tickets (e.g. in\n"
"compliance with a \"Right to Forget\" request under the GDPR), you can use\n"
"the data privacy functions in the admin panel under *System > Data "
"Privacy*\n"
"or [use the console](/en/reference/console#deleting-records).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:28
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Chat sessions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:32
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Once a chat session has been marked **closed**, it is scheduled for "
"automatic deletion 12 months later."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:36
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"IP address logs for chat sessions can be manually deleted by following the "
"steps in the [chat section in the "
"console](/en/reference/console#remove-ip-address-logs)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CTI caller log"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:41
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The caller log shows only the 60 most recent entries. Each entry in the "
"caller log is automatically deleted after 12 months."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:42
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Log files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:45
#, markdown-text
msgid "Zammad writes log files to disk (typically under `/opt/zammad/log/`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:49
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Package installations will set up a separate system utility called "
"`logrotate` to rename and archive (or *rotate*) log files on a nightly basis "
"and remove old logs after 14 days."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:53
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"If installing from source, it is strongly recommended to configure "
"`logrotate` or a similar log management utility; Zammad will not purge old "
"logs on its own."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:54
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "User sessions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:58
#, markdown-text
msgid "Zammad maintains session information about every user currently logged in."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:63
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This information is automatically purged when a user logs out, and can\n"
"be viewed or manually deleted via the admin panel (under *System >\n"
"Sessions*). Users may also delete their own session information via the\n"
"user preferences menu, under *Devices*.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:66
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Session information includes IP address (and possibly geographic location), "
"browser, time of original login, and time of last visit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:67
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Data Privacy Tasks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:71
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Each entry in the data privacy task list is automatically deleted after 12 "
"months."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:72
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "External Services"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:78
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Zammad utilizes third-party web services for certain functions, meaning\n"
"that user data may occasionally be sent or exposed to third parties.\n"
"These functions can be individually disabled in the admin panel under\n"
"*Settings > System > Services*.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:87
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"> [!NOTE]\n"
"> By default, the third-party services that Zammad relies on are mostly\n"
"> ones hosted and managed by the Zammad Foundation itself, but Zammad\n"
"> can be extended to interface with other services instead.\n"
">\n"
"> The source code for these third-party service integrations can be\n"
"> found\n"
"> [here](https://github.com/zammad/zammad/tree/develop/lib/service).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:88
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Images"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:92
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"No private images or personally-identifying information are stored on "
"images.zammad.com."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:98
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The Images service caches publicly-available images from sources like "
"Gravatar and serves them to the Zammad application as user avatars and "
"organization logos. These images are discovered using MD5 digests of user "
"email addresses and organization domain names. User avatars are cached for 7 "
"days; organization logos are cached for 30 days."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:99
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "GeoCalendar"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:102 src/en/reference/privacy.md:111
#, markdown-text
msgid "No user information is stored or cached on geo.zammad.com."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:107
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"As part of its service-level agreement (SLA) functionality, Zammad requires "
"detailed, localized calendar information (*e.g.,* to set the time zone and "
"accommodate national holidays and daylight savings time).  The GeoCalendar "
"service is used to retrieve this information."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:108
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "GeoIP"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:117
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"One of Zammad's security features is to track user sessions based on the "
"user's browser and country of origin. Suspicious login activity from a "
"different browser or country may trigger Zammad to dispatch an alert email "
"to the affected user. The GeoIP service is used to associate IP addresses to "
"a geographic origin."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:118
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Geolocation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/privacy.md:125
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Zammad's geolocation service relies on OpenStreetMap (OSM) unless you turned "
"it off. If you provide an address (or parts of an address) in a user object, "
"there is a lookup of coordinates from OSM which are stored in Zammad's "
"database. Have a look at their [privacy "
"policy](https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy) for more information."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:12
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Some attributes of articles might not be straight forward or come with "
"fairly many options - below list hopefully helps you on this journey."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:13
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`content_type`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:18
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Zammad supports `text/html` for HTML formatted text or `text/plain` for "
"plain text. This allows you to have better formatting options if you need "
"them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:20
#, markdown-text
msgid "Zammad web UI usually uses `text/html`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:21
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`type`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:26
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Zammad supports a huge number of article types. Below list may be incomplete "
"depending on your instance and possibly installed add-ons / custom changes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:29
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"If not stated otherwise, all article types below are **communication "
"articles** and thus affecting SLA calculation in Zammad defaults."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:33
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The difference is that communication articles provide the option to reply "
"automatically. Which actions exactly are available depends on the article "
"type and e.g. recipient lists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`email`\n"
": This allows you to create incoming or outgoing email articles.\n"
"  However, this highly depends on the chosen `sender`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:40
#, markdown-text
msgid "`phone` : Indicates phone notes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:44
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`web`\n"
": Usually used by customers only. This type is being used when ever your\n"
"  customer uses the web UI to create articles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:48
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`note`\n"
": When ever a communication does not fit (e.g.: internal notes) choose\n"
"  note. Zammad also uses this article type as default fall back.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:50
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  This is **not a communication article**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:53
#, markdown-text
msgid "`sms` : This type is being used for Zammad's SMS integration."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:57
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`chat`\n"
": This article type is technically a place holder and is only available\n"
"  via API.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:61
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`fax`\n"
": This article type is technically a place holder and is only available\n"
"  via API.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:66
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`twitter status` & `twitter direct-message`\n"
": These articles types are used by Zammad's twitter channel. Technically\n"
"  you can use these to automatically respond to existing requests via\n"
"  twitter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:71
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`facebook feed post` & `facebook feed comment`\n"
": These articles types are used by Zammad's facebook channel. Technically\n"
"  you can use these to automatically respond to existing requests via\n"
"  facebook.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:75
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`telegram personal-message`\n"
": Used by Zammad's Telegram channel. Technically you can use these to\n"
"  automatically respond to existing requests via Telegram.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:76
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`internal`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:81
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"This attribute allows you to set the visibility of your articles. For "
"internal visible only use `true`, for visibly for your customers as well use "
"`false`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (warning)
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:82
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Visibility: internal doesn't mean it's silent**\n"
"\n"
"If you set an article to `internal: true` but choose to send an email,\n"
"please be aware that said Email is still being sent out!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:89
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`sender`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/rest-api/articles.md:92
#, markdown-text
